            <ArticleTitle>Effectiveness of indomethacin (indocid) suppositories as post-episiotomy analgesia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>57-60</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A double-blind, randomised placebo-controlled study was carried out to assess the efficacy of indometacin suppositories as a post-episiotomy analgesic. Thirty patients received 2 X 100 mg indometacin suppositories, and 30 patients received placebo suppositories within 15 min of an episiotomy repair operation. Local infiltration of the episiotomy incision was performed using 20 ml of 0.5% lignocaine in every case. Subjective symptoms of pain were evaluated 15, 30, 60 and 90 min post-episiotomy repair. Average numbers of hours as lying in patients after delivery is 4 h. None of the patients who received indometacin complained of post-episiotomy pain; whereas the patients on placebo manifested varying degrees of pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
